.TH JUKE 1 "2026-07-12" "jukebox 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
juke \- Apple Music for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B juke
[\fIcommand\fR] [\fIquery\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A three-panel TUI remote control for Music.app or Cider on macOS. The player
app keeps doing the playing; juke drives Music.app over
.BR osascript ,
or Cider over its local HTTP API, renders cover art as real pixels via the
Kitty graphics protocol, and shows time-synced lyrics. Run with no command to
open the TUI. See
.B BACKENDS
for how one is chosen.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B play [\fIquery\fR]
Pick a song and play it. With no query, resume playback.
.TP
.B queue [\fIquery\fR]
Pick songs to play next — appended to juke's own queue on Music.app (see
.BR QUEUE ),
or to Cider's. With no query, show the current track and what follows it.
.TP
.B play \-q <\fIquery\fR>
Same as
.BR queue .
.TP
.B album <\fIquery\fR>
Pick an album, play it in order.
.TP
.B artist <\fIquery\fR>
Pick an artist, play everything by them.
.TP
.B playlist <\fIquery\fR>
Pick a playlist, play it.
.TP
.B search <\fIquery\fR>
List matching songs without playing.
.TP
.B status
Print what is playing: track, position, volume, shuffle and repeat. Works
against whichever backend is active and needs no terminal.
.TP
.B pause
Toggle play/pause.
.TP
.B next\fR, \fBprev
Skip to the next / previous track. Walks juke's queue when one is active,
Music's own transport otherwise.
.TP
.B shuffle
Toggle shuffle.
.TP
.B repeat
Cycle repeat. Music.app goes off \(-> all \(-> one; Cider exposes only a
toggle, so there it goes none \(-> one \(-> all.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable output for
.BR status ", " search " and bare " queue .
Accepted in any position, and stripped before the query is assembled so it
cannot be searched for. See
.B JSON OUTPUT
below.
.SH JSON OUTPUT
With
.BR \-\-json ,
stdout carries exactly one JSON value and nothing else; errors stay on stderr
with a non-zero exit status.
.TP
.B juke status \-\-json
One object:
.IR backend " (\fBmusic\fR or \fBcider\fR), " state ", "
.IR volume ", " shuffle ", " repeat ", and " track .
.I track
is
.B null
when nothing is loaded, and otherwise
.IR "{id, name, artist, album, duration, position, year, favourite}" ,
with times in seconds. Test
.I track
rather than
.IR state :
a stopped player still reports a volume and a repeat mode.
.TP
.B juke search \fIquery\fB \-\-json
An array of
.IR "{id, name, artist, album}" ,
empty when nothing matches.
.TP
.B juke queue \-\-json
One object:
.IR playing ", " up " and " approximate .
.I playing
is
.B null
when nothing is loaded. When
.I approximate
is true the order is a guess, because shuffle reshapes the backend's own
context and the list cannot be read exactly.
.SH BACKENDS
Music.app is the default. Cider 4 is used instead when
.B JUKEBOX_CIDER_TOKEN
is set
.I and
Cider's local API answers; otherwise juke falls back to Music.app silently.
With no token nothing is probed, so Music.app users pay no startup cost.
.B JUKEBOX_PLAYER
overrides the choice, and
.B JUKEBOX_PLAYER=cider
with no reachable Cider is an error rather than a silent downgrade.
.PP
The backends differ in five visible ways: Cider uses its own queue instead of
the file queue and watcher below, its library arrives over HTTP (a couple of
seconds, during which the browser says
.IR "loading library\[u2026]" ),
its lyrics are Apple's own rather than lrclib's, it reports no play count,
and
.B search
returns at most 25 matches there against 100 on Music.app.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B JUKEBOX_CIDER_TOKEN
Cider's External Application API token (Cider \(-> Settings \(->
Connectivity). Setting it enables the Cider backend.
.TP
.B JUKEBOX_PLAYER
.BR cider " or " music :
pick the backend by hand.
.SH TUI KEYS
.B j/k
or arrows move,
.B enter
play,
.B l
open album/playlist,
.B h
back,
.B a
add to queue,
.B 1\-4
switch tabs,
.B tab
cycles preview/lyrics/queue.
In the queue view:
.B enter
play,
.B x
remove,
.B J/K
reorder.
.B /
filter,
.B esc
clear,
.B space
pause,
.B \(<-/\(->
prev/next,
.B +/\-
volume,
.B s
shuffle,
.B r
repeat,
.B q
quit.
.SH QUEUE
This section is about the Music.app backend only; Cider has a real queue and
uses it.
.PP
Music.app never exposed its real Up Next to scripting, so juke keeps its own:
.I ~/.cache/jukebox/queue.json
plus a detached watcher process (spawned automatically by any play or queue
operation) that starts the next queued track just before the current one
ends. The queue survives closing the terminal; the watcher exits when the
queue runs out, or stands down if you start playing something else yourself.
Playlists picked with
.B juke playlist
play natively through Music.app instead.
.PP
Trade-offs: no gapless playback between queued tracks, and Music's
shuffle/repeat settings do not apply to the queue \(em it always plays in
order.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.cache/jukebox/
Cover art, lyrics, the queue file, and the watcher pidfile. Regenerable;
safe to delete.
.SH NOTES
Lyrics come from Apple's own time-synced lyrics on Cider, and from lrclib.net
otherwise (which sends title/artist/duration when the lyrics view is open).
macOS only.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR osascript (1)
